  "notes": "One narrow window only, exactly as the research frames it: the 1998 pitch-processed europop record that reset pop vocal production. About 133 straight with the backbeat on 2&4 over four-on-the-floor, so the kick anchors all four beats with `syncopation` down at 0.06 — the flattest kick in the section. The other two drum deltas are quoted: \"open hat on every off-8th\" (a 0.85 probability across all four \"&\"s, two to four per bar) and \"16th shaker\" plus \"filtered-noise riser and a bar of kick-only as the pre-chorus device\", which is the shaker/riser perc block and a very high `dropOutBeats`. The harmony is the entry's standout fact and is encoded whole: a documented eight-chord cycle, I–V–iii–IV–I–vi–iii–vii, which is why `maxChords` is 8 — an unusually long loop for a dance-pop record and the reason this model does not sound like the rest of the europop bench. The hook is \"the *processed voice itself* — Auto-Tune set to extreme retune speed, removing portamento so pitch moves in steps\", which is encoded the only way a note generator can say it: a staccato articulation, a very high `chordToneBias` and a two-bar repeated chorus cell inside the stated F-sharp3–C-sharp5 range. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are the drum probabilities and the melody density; the entry names the range and the cell length, not a note count.",
